1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:11,266
You are a wet river boulder right on the edge of Brooks Falls in Alaska.

2
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:33,304
Feel that roar all around your ears. Millions of gallons of cold river water rush past you every hour.

3
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,709
Watch the white spray straight ahead — silver sockeye salmon are jumping right up the waterfall.

4
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,939
Straight ahead. Wait for it.

5
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:35,666
A giant brown bear stands right on the top edge, holding as still as a statue with his mouth wide open.

6
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,250
Snap! A jumping salmon lands right in his jaws before it can clear the crest.

7
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,042
In summer, one big bear can eat more than thirty salmon in a single day to fatten up for winter.

8
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,908
Now turn behind you to your right and look down into the bubbly white pool people call the jacuzzi.

9
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,276
Other bears sit chest-deep in the swirling foam, dunking their heads underwater like snorkelers.

10
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,455
Only the biggest, strongest bear gets to stand on the top lip above.

11
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:55,845
Now look over to the shallow gravel edge on your left. A mother bear has brought two fuzzy cubs.

12
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,435
She is teaching them, but from a safe distance.

13
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,419
Big male bears can be rough, so she fishes in the quiet shallows where her cubs can watch every move.

14
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:02,380
All July long, the salmon keep leaping upstream, feeding the whole forest.

15
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,829
See who is standing on the falls right now on the Brooks Falls brown bears live cam at Digital Live Zoo.
